WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.450
Deleting data is not always the same as making a row
inactive

00:00:04.338 --> 00:00:08.362
This slide compares a soft delete with a hard delete
A soft

00:00:08.388 --> 00:00:11.600
delete keeps the row in the database but changes a
status field

00:00:12.462 --> 00:00:16.175
In our example we use UPDATE to set is_active to
false for

00:00:16.188 --> 00:00:21.025
a specific patient The row still exists so its
history and relationships

00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:24.875
can remain available but the application can treat
the record as inactive

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:29.012
A hard delete uses DELETE FROM and physically
removes a row from

00:00:29.038 --> 00:00:33.250
the table That may be appropriate in some situations
but it can

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:38.375
also remove important history or conflict with
related records The example on

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.788
the slide demonstrates a soft delete We target the
patient using a

00:00:42.812 --> 00:00:47.500
unique MRN and use RETURNING to verify the new
active status Run

00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:51.788
the statement and inspect the returned row Notice
that the patient still

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.725
has an ID MRN and name Only the active flag has
changed

00:00:57.588 --> 00:01:00.950
In real systems the choice between soft and hard
delete depends on

00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:06.888
business rules retention requirements audit needs
and privacy policies The technical

00:01:06.912 --> 00:01:11.362
SQL command is only part of the decision For this
course remember

00:01:11.388 --> 00:01:15.312
the practical distinction soft delete preserves the
row and changes its status

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.338
hard delete removes the row itself
